   "subject": "Re: changing features in line plots",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi:\r<br>\r<br>Does this approximate what you&#39;re after?\r<br>\r<br>library(ggplot2)\r<br>\r<br>ggplot(new.data, aes(x = years, y = value, linetype = type))+\r<br>  geom_line(size = 1)+\r<br>  scale_linetype_manual(name=&quot;Forecast Type&quot;,\r<br>                        values=c(&quot;solid&quot;, &quot;dashed&quot;),\r<br>                        labels = c(&quot;Box-Cox&quot;, &quot;e&quot;)) +\r<br>  theme(strip.background = element_rect(fill = &quot;white&quot;),\r<br>        strip.text.x = element_text(size = rel(1.4))) +\r<br>  facet_wrap(~ area, ncol = 3)\r<br>\r<br>To modify non-data aspects of a ggplot, such as the appearance of the\r<br>facet strips, use the theming system. You can see the elements and the\r<br>default values of their properties by typing &#39;theme_grey&#39; at the\r<br>prompt (without the quotes). In this case, the specification &#39;size =\r<br>rel(1.4)&#39;  in strip.text.x increases the default strip text size by\r<br>40%.\r<br>\r<br>The values argument of a scale function refers to the values of the\r<br>aesthetic to be plotted in the graphic, while the labels argument\r<br>refers to the labels to be placed in the legend. Implicitly, the\r<br>breaks argument refers to the levels of the factor mapped to the\r<br>aesthetic, which in this case are &#39;boxcox&#39; and &#39;fcast&#39;. Therefore,\r<br>level &#39;boxcox&#39; is associated with a solid line and legend label\r<br>&#39;Box-Cox&#39;, whereas level &#39;fcast&#39; is associated with a dashed line and\r<br>legend label &#39;e&#39;.\r<br>\r<br>Dennis\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
